## Why This Is Asked
This tests minimax-style DP with reconstruction for a classic game.

## Key Concepts
- Two-player DP with difference state
- Subarray DP and reconstruction path
- Time: O(n^2) space: O(n^2)

## Code Example
```javascript
// dp[i][j] denotes max diff for i..j
function maxFirstPlayer(values){
  const n = values.length; const dp = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  let total = values.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][i]=values[i];
  for(let len=2; len<=n; len++){
    for(let i=0;i+len-1<n;i++){
      const j=i+len-1;
      dp[i][j] = Math.max(values[i]-dp[i+1][j], values[j]-dp[i][j-1]);
    }
  }
  return (total + dp[0][n-1])/2;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the exact L/R sequence from the DP table?
- How does this change if moves include taking from both ends with a coin flip to decide who starts?

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This problem combines prefix DP with an inner backward sweep to compute the optimal segment ending at each position. It tests understanding of state decomposition, reconstruction logic, and handles edge cases like negative values naturally.

## Key Concepts
- DP state: dp[i][t] tracks the optimal value using the first i elements with t segments
- Inner loop: expanding the left boundary computes segment maximum on the fly
- Reconstruction: store predecessor pointers to recover exact subarray boundaries
- Complexity: O(n²k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueSubarrays(A, k) {
    const n = A.length;
    const dp = Array(n + 1).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
    const prev = Array(n + 1).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
    dp[0][0] = 0;

    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
            let curMax = -Infinity;
            for (let l = i; l >= 1; l--) {
                curMax = Math.max(curMax, A[l - 1]);
                const val = (dp[l - 1][t - 1] !== -Infinity ? dp[l - 1][t - 1] + curMax : -Infinity);
                if (val > dp[i][t]) {
                    dp[i][t] = val;
                    prev[i][t] = l;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    const result = [];
    let i = n, t = k;
    while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
        const l = prev[i][t];
        result.unshift([l, i]);
        i = l - 1;
        t--;
    }
    return { value: dp[n][k], boundaries: result };
}
```

