## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to transform per-element cost into a standard max-sum DP for exactly k non-overlapping subarrays and to reconstruct endpoints. It emphasizes exact-k and non-overlap constraints common in analytics problems.

## Key Concepts
- Transform to B[i] = A[i] - c and reduce to max-sum of k non-overlapping subarrays
- DP states: local[i][t] for subarray ending at i; glob[i][t] for best so far with t subarrays
- Recurrences and reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(B, k) {
  const n = B.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const local = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const glob  = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  local[0][0] = 0; glob[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      local[i][t] = Math.max(local[i-1][t] + B[i-1], glob[i-1][t-1] + B[i-1]);
      glob[i][t] = Math.max(glob[i-1][t], local[i][t]);
    }
  }
  return {max: glob[n][k], endpoints: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the exact endpoints efficiently?
- How would you adapt if at-most-k subarrays were allowed instead of exactly k?

## Why This Is Asked
This gauges ability to cast a line-wrapping task into a clear 1D DP with a non-linear line cost and a reconstruction path. It tests handling of last-line quirks and feasibility checks.

## Key Concepts
- 1D DP over word indices; dp[n] = 0 as base.
- Prefix sums to compute line width in O(1) per extension.
- Cost function uses squared unused space; last line excluded.
- Reconstruct solution via next indices.
- Time: O(n^2); Space: O(n).

## Code Example
```javascript
function minWrap(words, W) {
  const n = words.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pref[i + 1] = pref[i] + words[i];
  const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(Infinity);
  const nxt = new Array(n).fill(-1);
  dp[n] = 0;
  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      const width = (pref[j + 1] - pref[i]) + (j - i);
      if (width <= W) {
        const cost = (W - width) * (W - width) + dp[j + 1];
        if (cost < dp[i]) { dp[i] = cost; nxt[i] = j + 1; }
      } else break;
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return { minPenalty: dp[0], next: nxt };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you modify to penalize the last line as well?
- How would you incorporate tie-breaking to prefer fewer lines?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP design with an extra edit dimension on a classic string problem; requires careful transitions for matches, edits, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on substrings (i, j)
- Extra dimension for edits (t)
- Transitions for match/mismatch with edits
- Path reconstruction and complexity trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxLPSWithEdits(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0)));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) for (let t=0;t<=k;t++) dp[i][i][t] = 1;
  for (let len=2; len<=n; len++) {
    for (let i=0; i+len-1<n; i++) {
      const j = i+len-1;
      for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) {
        let best = Math.max(dp[i+1][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t]);
        if (s[i] === s[j]) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t] + 2);
        if (t >= 1) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t-1] + 2);
        if (t >= 2) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t-2] + 2);
        dp[i][j][t] = best;
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0][n-1][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to require exactly k edits instead of at most?
- How to reduce space to O(n*k)?
- How to reconstruct the edited string and the subsequence?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a nontrivial DP for partitioning with a per-block cost derived from a range statistic, plus reconstruction across k blocks.

## Key Concepts
- Precompute cost[l][r] = max(A[l..r])*(r-l+1) in O(n^2).
- DP: dp[i][t] = max_{l≤i} dp[l-1][t-1] + cost[l][i].
- Track par[i][t] for block endpoints; base dp[i][1] = cost[0][i].
- Space/time tradeoffs; discuss optimizations (rolling arrays, divide-and-conquer). 

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxBlockPartition(a, k) {
  const n = a.length;
  const cost = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let m = -Infinity;
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      m = Math.max(m, a[r]);
      cost[l][r] = m * (r - l + 1);
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = cost[0][i];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        const val = dp[l-1][t-1] + cost[l][i];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = l-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return {dp, par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement a faster optimization (e.g., divide-and-conquer) if opt indices are monotone?
- How would you adapt to allow at most k blocks or variable block values (e.g., using different per-block metrics)?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests deriving a solvable DP for contiguous partitions with a length-based cost, plus a linear-time optimization and path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- Contiguous partition DP
- Prefix sums and O(nk) optimization
- Backtracking to report segment endpoints

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxTotalWithCost(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    let bestVal = -Infinity, bestIdx = -1;
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      const j = i - 1;
      if (dp[j][t-1] > -Infinity) {
        const cand = dp[j][t-1] - S[j] + c * j;
        if (cand > bestVal) { bestVal = cand; bestIdx = j; }
      }
      if (bestVal > -Infinity) {
        dp[i][t] = S[i] - c * i + c + bestVal;
        par[i][t] = bestIdx;
      }
    }
  }
  const segments = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  if (dp[n][k] <= -Infinity/2) return {best: -Infinity, segments: []};
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = par[i][t];
    segments.push([j+1, i]);
    i = j; t--;
  }
  segments.reverse();
  return {best: dp[n][k], segments};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt if c is paid only for non-first element or per segment boundary?
- How would you extend to maximize total value for at most k segments instead of exactly k?

## How It Works

Concise technical explanation of the mechanism.

## Key Points

- **DP State**: local[k], global[k] track ending vs non-ending subarrays
- **Transition**: local[k] = max(local[k]+a[i], global[k-1]+a[i]); global[k] = max(global[k], local[k])
- **Edge Handling**: cap K to n; initialize -inf; handles negative values

## Example

```javascript
function maxRevenue(A, K) {
  const n = A.length;
  if (n === 0 || K <= 0) return 0;
  K = Math.min(K, n);
  const global = new Array(K+1).fill(-Infinity);
  const local = new Array(K+1).fill(-Infinity);
  global[0] = 0;
  for (let x of A) {
    for (let k = K; k >= 1; k--) {
      local[k] = Math.max((local[k] !== -Infinity ? local[k] + x : -Infinity), global[k-1] + x);
      global[k] = Math.max(global[k], local[k]);
    }
  }
  return global[K];
}
```
